# Public Expo env vars are inlined into the bundle at build time.
# Change them, then restart the dev server (expo start --clear) for them to apply.
#
# `EXPO_PUBLIC_*` values are compiled into the JS bundle. They are NOT secrets --
# anyone with the APK can read them back out. Never put a key or token here.
#
# --- Which backend URL to use -------------------------------------------------
#
# Hosted backend (default): http://185.239.0.14:1243/lesan
#
# Running the backend locally instead (`cd ../back && deno task start`, port 1405):
#   Android emulator: the host machine is 10.0.2.2, not localhost
#                     -> http://10.0.2.2:1405/lesan
#   Physical device:  use your machine's LAN IP, and put the phone on the same Wi-Fi
#                     -> http://192.168.1.20:1405/lesan
#
# Note that the hosted backend is plain HTTP. Android 9+ blocks cleartext by default,
# which is why `app.json` sets `expo-build-properties` -> `usesCleartextTraffic: true`.
# Credentials and auth tokens therefore travel unencrypted over the public internet;
# put the backend behind HTTPS (a reverse proxy with a certificate) before real use.
#
# --- Cloud builds (EAS) -------------------------------------------------------
#
# `.env` is gitignored, and EAS archives the project using `.gitignore`, so a cloud
# build NEVER sees this file. It reads `eas.json` instead -- which is why all three
# build profiles there repeat `EXPO_PUBLIC_LESAN_URL` in their `env` block.
#
# That is the documented home for it. Expo's eas.json reference says `env` is "only
# ... for values that you would commit to your git repository and not for passwords
# or secrets", which is exactly true of an EXPO_PUBLIC_ value: it is inlined into the
# bundle and readable by anyone holding the APK.
#
# So change the backend URL in `eas.json` (all three profiles), not here. A plain
# `npm run build:apk` then picks it up with no dashboard step to forget. Everything
# below only affects `expo start` and local runs.
#
# The alternative is an EAS environment variable per environment:
#   npx --yes eas-cli@latest env:set --name EXPO_PUBLIC_LESAN_URL \
#     --value http://185.239.0.14:1243/lesan --environment preview --visibility plaintext
# Use one or the other, never both: the precedence between a profile's `env` and an
# EAS environment variable of the same name is not documented, so setting both turns
# the effective value into guesswork.
EXPO_PUBLIC_LESAN_URL=http://185.239.0.14:1243/lesan

# Only two values are allowed (see `src/constants/env.ts`). This controls a single
# thing: the `console.warn("[lesan]", ...)` diagnostic gate in `src/lib/client.ts`.
# Use `development` to keep API-failure logs while testing; `production` silences them.
EXPO_PUBLIC_APP_ENV=development
